WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.935
Validation asks whether the Bayesian network behaves well enough for its intended purpose.

00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:08.282
Compare predictions with observed outcomes when data is available.

00:00:08.282 --> 00:00:09.398
Check probability calibration:

00:00:09.398 --> 00:00:13.861
when the model says an event has about a 70 percent chance,

00:00:13.861 --> 00:00:19.812
events in that range should happen roughly 70 percent of the time over many comparable cases.

00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:22.044
Calibration is not about one case.

00:00:22.044 --> 00:00:25.019
It is about probability quality across repeated use.

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:29.854
Sensitivity analysis asks how much outputs change when assumptions or probability values change.

00:00:29.854 --> 00:00:34.317
If a small change in one CPT row dramatically changes a decision,

00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:35.805
that row deserves review.

00:00:35.805 --> 00:00:37.664
Test unusual and boundary scenarios.

00:00:37.664 --> 00:00:39.896
Review whether important variables are missing.

00:00:39.896 --> 00:00:44.359
Check probability sources and document whether they came from data, expert judgment,

00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:46.591
literature, simulation, or a temporary assumption.

00:00:46.591 --> 00:00:48.078
Bayesian networks have limits.

00:00:48.078 --> 00:00:50.310
Poor data can produce poor estimates.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.541
Incorrect graph assumptions can distort inference.

00:00:52.541 --> 00:00:55.145
Hidden common causes can create misleading relationships.

00:00:55.145 --> 00:00:57.748
Rare events may be hard to estimate.

00:00:57.748 --> 00:00:59.980
Human probability estimates can be inconsistent.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.071
A model may perform differently for different populations, environments, product versions,

00:01:04.071 --> 00:01:05.187
or operating conditions.

00:01:05.187 --> 00:01:08.534
Outputs can appear more certain than the evidence justifies.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:10.022
Responsible use requires maintenance.

00:01:10.022 --> 00:01:11.138
Track model versions.

00:01:11.138 --> 00:01:12.997
Monitor data and environment changes.

00:01:12.997 --> 00:01:15.229
Reassess the network when relationships change.

00:01:15.229 --> 00:01:18.576
Document purpose, owners, assumptions, limitations, review criteria, missing-data handling,

00:01:18.576 --> 00:01:19.692
and escalation paths.

00:01:19.692 --> 00:01:23.411
For consequential decisions, use human oversight and appropriate domain review.

00:01:23.411 --> 00:01:26.015
A Bayesian network can support decision making,

00:01:26.015 --> 00:01:29.362
but it should not be treated as an oracle.

00:01:29.362 --> 00:01:34.197
It is a model whose usefulness depends on fit, evidence, validation, and governance.

00:01:34.197 --> 00:01:37.544
Calibration and sensitivity work should be understandable to stakeholders.

00:01:37.544 --> 00:01:41.264
A technical team may inspect error metrics and posterior distributions,

00:01:41.264 --> 00:01:47.215
while a manager may need to know whether the model is reliable enough for a workflow.

00:01:47.215 --> 00:01:48.330
Both views matter.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:53.165
Responsible reporting should explain where the model performs well, where it is uncertain,

00:01:53.165 --> 00:01:55.769
and which inputs most affect the result.

00:01:55.769 --> 00:01:59.860
That makes the model easier to govern and safer to improve.
